The most effective leverage point is reducing total system pressure on shared environmental resources (the commons).
Why this is high impact
This directly targets the root of the problem:
not farm size alone
but how farming practices collectively impact shared resources
If Ontario reduces pressure on soil and water systems, it:
slows the commons depletion loop (R2)
increases long-term productivity
improves resilience across all farms
What loops it affects
Weakens R2 (resource depletion)
Slows R1 (intensification pressure)
Strengthens long-term sustainability
How to act on it
tie funding to sustainable practices
reward efficiency per unit of resource, not just output
support technologies that reduce input intensity
encourage distributed production across farms
Risks and resistance
large farms may resist constraints
short-term output may decline slightly
measuring environmental impact can be complex